{"ok": true, "summary": "Root cause: the dynamic dict (`src/dynpy/rt_core.rs`) probed its HashMap while holding the dict's `RefCell` borrow, so a key's user `__eq__` that mutates the dict (e.g. `d.clear()`) re-entered the RefCell and panicked with "RefCell already borrowed".

Fix: make `Dict.idx` an `Rc<HashMap>` (copy-on-write via `Rc::make_mut`). For keys whose `__eq__` may run user code (tracked per-dict by a new `unsafe_eq` count; builtin/int/str/tuple/identity-object keys keep the old single-borrow fast path), new `*_cell` operations for get/contains/set/setdefault/del/pop/getitem/move_to_end now: snapshot the Rc and `ver` under a short borrow, run the probe (identity then user `__eq__`) with no borrow held, re-borrow to check `ver`, and restart if it changed — bounded at 200 restarts with `RuntimeError("dict mutated during lookup")`. Mutations applied after a successful probe use an identity-only comparison thread-local so the second HashMap probe under the borrow cannot call user code. `clear`/`copy`/`compact`/`move_to_end` were adjusted for the Rc index and `unsafe_eq` bookkeeping, and all dict-probe call sites in rt_ops/rt_lib/rt_mod/rt_enum/pyglue now use the cell variants so no borrow is held across user code. Also fixed a stale-index bug my `compact` rewrite exposed in `move_to_end`, and made a Counter `__add__` loop release its borrow.

Tested: `check_test` now matches CPython on both normal and forced dynamic builds for `test_clear_at_lookup`; the sister `test_dict_contain_use_after_free` script also matches both builds. Manually verified nearby behaviour via try_python: eq raising/clearing during get/set/setdefault/del/pop/contains/views, identity-only and tuple/dataclass keys, hash-mutating keys, dict equality, Counter ops, copy with custom keys (no extra __eq__/__hash__), compaction order, and OrderedDict.move_to_end."}
